## Context

Child of 0700. **Build early**, before drafting starts: a guard that only runs
at submission flags an overlap when it is expensive to undo, instead of
preventing it when it arises.

The author declared to the editor, for transparency, that this review and the
Œconomia manuscript would have **no text or analysis in common**: the two use
the same corpus in opposite ways. The history paper asks how the object was
assembled and uses the corpus only to corroborate its periodisation; the review
maps and synthesises the research literature itself, which the history paper
does not attempt.

A promise that no test checks decays at the first rushed commit. This ticket
makes it mechanical.

## Actions

1. Compare the two **rendered** texts (not the sources: markup hides reuse) by
   detecting shared n-grams, with a declared threshold.
2. Declare, in a configuration file, which analysis scripts each manuscript may
   use, and fail on any crossing.
3. Wire the guard into the review's build target, not only into the test suite:
   it must run at every render.

## Test

**The guard is proven on a known positive case**: copying a paragraph of
Œconomia into the review draft must turn it red. A guard whose "found nothing"
is indistinguishable from "I could not look" is not a guard — the bench naming
policy supplied the counter-example on 2026-08-21, when the first scan returned
"clean" with the leak in plain view.

Also check the false positive: the two manuscripts legitimately share
institution names, titles of cited works and the description of the corpus. The
threshold must tolerate them, and the formal citation of the same source is not
reuse.

## Invariants

- The guard compares texts, it does not arbitrate: a flagged overlap is read and
  settled by hand.
- It must not require both manuscripts to be built together when one does not
  change.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Guard written, threshold declared and justified
- [ ] Proven on a purpose-built positive case, and on the legitimate false positives
- [ ] Wired into the review's render
- [ ] Green on the current state of both manuscripts
